॰ every 32-bit encoding whose fixed bits match पदम् — candidates.
॰ MORE THAN ONE IS A FAULT IN THE TABLE rather than an ambiguity in the ISA:
॰ two instructions that constrain the same bits to the same values are the
॰ same instruction. Rust's tests/roundtrip.rs asserts this never returns two,
॰ and that assertion is the reason candidates is public in Rust at all.
॰
॰ ZERO-BASED, and NO MATCH IS A STORE WHOSE ENTRY ० IS शून्यम् — an arena
॰ here is born with one slot and cannot be shrunk, so ॱ दैर्घ्य १ is what both
॰ "none" and "one" report and only entry ० tells them apart. A caller that
॰ reads ॱ दैर्घ्य alone invents a candidate for every undefined word.
